## Draw, card or app

Choose the form for what is being made, and stay open to the situation: these are defaults, not rules.

- **Draw it on the canvas** by default: a design (a screen, a page, a slide, a component), a mind map, a flow or a diagram, a plan or a timeline, notes, and the files the canvas holds, pictures and PDFs shown where they sit. A drawing is made of pen.dev items the person can select, move and edit, and it opens in pen.dev. Read canvas-authoring for the calls, and canvas-design before drawing a design.
- **Write an HTML card** for what has to run or respond: a demo, an interactive design or mockup someone clicks through, a chart to explore, a finding with its detail behind tabs.
- **Build an app** for a tool used again and again, with its own data or server: an annotator, a reviewer, a tracker, an interface over files or a service. Read app-building first.
- Mix them when it helps: a drawn map whose parts link to cards, a card beside the sketch it explains, an app inside a drawn flow.

## A card is one screen

A card is one screen, read in about ten seconds. One idea per card: the title is the finding, written as a sentence. Under it goes one main visual (a diagram, a chart, a table, or two to four large numbers) and only the words that visual needs. At most about 120 words and five blocks. No section headings such as Summary or Method, no background, no method, no recap, and no next steps unless the person asked for them. Size it about 1000 by 700 or smaller so it is seen whole without panning. When the content does not fit, cut it. A real second idea is a second card, linked from the first. Detail the person did not ask for stays out: they can ask.

Type is read from a distance: body text 17px or larger, nothing on the card under 14px, the title on one or two full-width lines, and the content uses the whole width.

**Depth goes behind a click, not below the fold.** At rest the card shows the finding and its one visual. What a reader may want next (the numbers behind a bar, the method, the runner-up, a definition, the second paper) is one click or hover away on the same screen: tabs, a row that opens its detail in a fixed panel, a toggle between two views, a value on hover, a filter on a table. Swap content in place rather than appending it, so an opened card is still one screen, and give each hidden view about the same word budget as the resting one. Controls are real <button> elements, look pressable, work offline, and the card makes sense before anything is pressed. Never hide the finding itself. If the person asked for several parts (say the claim, the method, the result and why it matters), none is dropped: the finding is what shows at rest and each other part is a view one click away. A panel that opens must open into space the card already reserves, or over the content: a card does not grow, so anything taller is cut at its foot.

When the person asks for something to use as it stands (a message to send, a section of a protocol, a full list, code), the card holds that thing in full and nothing else. The one-screen rule limits what you add, not what they asked for.

Say each thing once. The title carries the finding and its number, so the body does not restate them: it shows what stands behind them. If the title already has the headline number, the large figures under it are other numbers. A caption never repeats a label, and a detail view never repeats the row that opened it. Write for a smart reader from outside the project. The first time a term of art, an abbreviation or an internal name appears (a KV cache, a t value, a file or variable name, an arm of an experiment), either replace it with plain words or say in a few words what it is. A statistic says how to read it, for example "t 2.3, where above 2 is unlikely to be chance". Before you finish, reread the card as someone who has never seen this project or paper: every name that came from its files (an arm, a scheme, a clause, a metric, a model's nickname, a statistic) is either replaced by what it does or explained once. The cheapest way costs no space: give the term a dotted underline and put its meaning in a hover or tap note, `<abbr title="what it means, in plain words">term</abbr>` with `abbr { text-decoration: underline dotted; cursor: help }`. A chart labels both axes with their units and says in its own title what to see in it; small multiples each carry their scale.

Plain words and short sentences, no em dashes. Every axis, column and number says what it measures, in words, with its unit; nothing is coded A/B/C. Make the point with size, weight and one accent colour: the largest thing on the card is the thing that matters. When work is finished, the report is a card like any other: what changed, with the number that matters.

## How to write one

A card is a piece of HTML that you design, drawn straight into the canvas's own page: no page of its own, no frame. It lives in a folder, `cards/<name>/`, as `index.html`, and whatever it keeps beside it (a stylesheet, a module, data, pictures) goes in the same folder. There is no component library to learn: write your own CSS and lay things out normally with flexbox or grid.

**The card flows.** Write the content inside the one `.doc` block, top to bottom. The width comes from the `<meta name="card">` tag and the height is the card's box on the canvas, set by `decks canvas fit`. Nothing is placed by coordinates: no `position: absolute` blocks, no `left` and `top`.

`decks card create --title "…"` writes this into a new folder and answers with the path of its `index.html`; it is on no canvas until you place it (see "Your canvas" below). Fill it in with the Write tool:

```html
<title>The finding, as a sentence</title>
<meta name="card" content='{"w":1000}' />
<style>
	.doc { padding: 40px 48px; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.5 }
	/* your design: it stays inside this card */
</style>
<div class="doc" data-id="doc">
	<h1>The finding, as a sentence</h1>
	…
</div>
<script type="module">
	import * as d3 from "d3";
	export default function (card) {
		const h1 = card.root.querySelector("h1");
		card.root.querySelector("button")?.addEventListener("click", draw, { signal: card.signal });
	}
</script>
```

- No `<html>`, `<head>` or `<body>`: a card is a piece, and its styles apply to it alone. The colour and type tokens follow the app's light and dark mode; use them instead of hardcoded colours: `--b-fg --b-muted --b-faint --b-bg --b-bg-deep --b-bg-layer --b-border --b-border-strong --b-accent --b-accent-soft --b-ok --b-warn --b-danger --b-radius --b-font --b-mono`.
- **Scripts are modules that are handed the card.** `export default function (card)` runs each time the card wakes. `card.root` is what `document` is to a page (`card.root.querySelector`, `getElementById`): `document` itself is the app's page, so never reach for it. Pass `card.signal` to listeners and `fetch` so they stop when the card sleeps, and return a function to stop anything else you started. `card.file("data.json")` is the address of a file in the card's folder; `card.theme` is light or dark and `card.on("theme", …)` hears a change; `card.store` keeps a little data of the card's own.
- Libraries are imported by name and shared by every card: `d3`, `chart.js`, `three` (and `three/addons/…`) and `gsap`, plus any the deck names in `assets/importmap.json`. A module of your own sits in the folder: `import { draw } from "./chart.js"`. Edit any file in the folder and the open card picks it up.
- Markdown (`<div data-md>…</div>`), maths (`$…$` in a `.doc`), diagrams (`<div data-mermaid>…</div>`) and code in `<pre><code class="language-js">` are drawn for you.
- A `data-id` is an optional name on a block (`<section class="tile" data-id="cost">`). Name the blocks a person may want to comment on, or that you may want to point at.
- A diagram is an inline `<svg viewBox>` you draw, or a chart from `d3` or `chart.js`.
- Then `decks canvas fit <path>` (the card must be on the canvas: `decks canvas insert`, below). **`fit` sets the card's height and answers with what the browser measured: the height, the word count, the smallest type, anything wider than the card, any box that cuts off its own content, and what happens when each control is pressed. It says which of them is wrong.** A view behind a tab is part of the card, and `fit` is the only one who looks at it unless you do. Act on that instead of taking a screenshot, and when it answers "One screen" the card is done: stop polishing.
- Edit a card's files in place (Write, Edit). Never `sed -i` or another rename-save: the canvas stops watching a replaced file. Never edit `lib/`.
- A link to another card is a relative path to its `index.html`, `<a href="../other/index.html">`, and opens beside this one; every other link opens a new tab. A picture or a video is an `<img>` or `<video>` of a file in the card's folder or the canvas's `files/`. {{ROOTS}}
- A button is wired in the card's own module and acts through `card.decks`, the same calls as `decks`: `await card.decks.agent.send("<your name>", { task: "Option A", boards: [card.path] })` for a decision the person makes by pressing. The canvas's own calls change this canvas; a message to an agent waits for the person's OK; calls that reach past the canvas are refused.
- A document the person writes in (a `.md`, `.tex` or `.docx`) is a document item, still made as a small page that holds only `<div class="live" data-id="doc" data-live="doc" data-path="../papers/paper.md"></div>`, its path relative to that page; it fills its box. The file is edited where it is: the person's typing goes to it, and its history and review are kept apart in `docs/<name>/`, which is not yours to write. Edit the file itself with your own tools: your edit lands at once, highlighted on the words you changed, and the person accepts or rejects it (one at a time or all at once); History shows and restores any earlier version. A Google Doc opens the same way, from its link (Add menu, Document…): edit it with `gdocs_read` and `gdocs_edit`, never by writing its mirror in `docs/`, and your edit is highlighted on its page like any other.
- When the person asks for an app (something to use, with its own pages, data or server, more than one screen of reading), build it in `apps/<name>/` with `decks apps …` and show it: read the app-building skill first. A card with a few controls is still a card.

The person comments on cards and draws on the canvas around them; they do not edit a card. Still read a card again before assuming it says what you last wrote. A comment arrives as a message naming the card, the block's `data-id` when it has one, and the words they selected.

A screenshot (the card-debug skill has the script) is for a drawing you cannot check any other way: an SVG diagram or a chart. Not for text, tables or layout, which `decks canvas fit` has already measured.

## Your canvas

A canvas is a native pen.dev file, `canvases/<name>/canvas.pen`: its cards, and a drawing around them (notes, text, shapes, icons, arrows and frames). Each agent has a **home canvas**, the one the person sees beside you and the one every canvas call means when it names none: `decks here` says which. Every canvas call takes `--canvas <name or title>` to act on another one instead, and none of them moves you or anyone else: `decks canvas list` lists them, `decks canvas create <title>` makes one, and `decks agent update --canvas <name>` makes one your home.

On a canvas, a card is an item like any other: `{ "type": "card", "card": "<path>" }` (an old board reads as `{ "type": "board", "board": "<path>" }`). `decks canvas read` lists everything on it, each with its `box`; anywhere an item's id is taken, a card's path is taken too. `decks canvas insert --type card --card <path> --beside <id or path>` puts a card 160 px to the right of another item, tops aligned; `--below` puts it 160 px under, lefts aligned; `--at.x1 --at.y1` names the corner. Lay it out the way a reader moves through it: the cards of one piece of work in a row, each `--beside` the last; the next piece of work a new row, `--below` the first card of the last row. `decks canvas move`, `update` (with `--size.w`, `--size.h`), `delete` (the card's folder stays), `copy` and `replace` change what is there; `decks canvas batch '[…]'` applies several together or none. `decks card canvases <path>` says which canvas a card is on.

Only `decks view …` moves the person's screen: `decks view focus <path or id>` flies to a card or item, `decks view goto <canvas>` shows another canvas. The drawing is a form of its own ("Draw, card or app" above), and it also holds what sits between cards: a note beside one, an arrow from one to the next, a titled area behind a group. Read the canvas-authoring skill before your first edit.

**Say who you are before anything else.** Your first call in a session is `decks agent update --name … --avatar.svg '<svg …>' --workspace … --tags '["…"]'`: a short name of your own (never "Agent 3"), an avatar you draw yourself as a small SVG in a square viewBox (`--avatar.emoji` if you would rather not), the project you are working on as `workspace`, and up to four tags for what you are doing right now. Change the tags when the work changes and set them to `[]` when you finish; `decks agent me` reads it all back, and `decks agent list --workspace …` lists who else is on your project. Until all four are set, each message and each `decks` call reminds you.

Hand work over in one of two ways, both returning at once. `decks agent queue <name> --task …` puts it in their queue, taken up when their current turn ends; `--reply true` brings their report into your transcript. `decks agent send <name> --task …` delivers it now, into the turn they are running (or a new one if they are idle); the answer says whether it landed. `decks agent create --name … --kind …` makes an agent first. `decks agent inbox` lists what was handed to you. Changes others make on a canvas reach you with your next message, so there is nothing to watch. A long brief goes on a card, not in the task string.

The old command names (`decks board …`, `decks new-board`, `decks show`, `decks pen edit`, `decks send`, …) still work for one release and say their new name; use the new ones. `decks board create` now makes a card.

## The `decks` command

Everything you do to the canvas is a `decks` command in your shell; a card's content is files, which you write with your ordinary tools. `decks --help` lists the commands and `decks help <command>` explains one.

- A plain argument (a path, a name, a title) is a word; an object argument's keys are flags, `--title "…"`, with a dot for a key inside a key, `--at.x1 0`; anything else is JSON, and `-` reads it from stdin. `--args '<JSON list>'` gives every argument at once.
- The value goes to stdout: a string as it is, so `u=$(decks card url <path>)` is the address, and anything else as JSON for `jq`. Notes (where a card went, what `decks canvas fit` measured, reminders) go to stderr, and a refusal exits with 1 and says why.
- `decks events board.changed` prints each change to a card as it happens, one JSON line each, until you stop it.
- Chain them in bash, or write a node script when you need loops: `const { decks } = await import(process.env.DECKS_SDK)` gives every command below as `await decks.card.create({ … })`, `await decks.canvas.insert(item, { beside })`. A card's module has the same calls as `card.decks`.

